{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# WIP technical design\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** MVP implemented for Claude Code ⇄ Codex; fabrication adapters are\n4\tversion-pinned and must be revalidated per CLI release.\n5\t\n6\t## Main flow\n7\t\n8\t1. Conversation Ledger's hooks (`cledger install all`) capture each CLI's\n9\t   local transcript incrementally into git notes as normalized\n10\t   `conversation_turn` events, stamped with the repo's git identity\n11\t   (`actor.id` = `user.email`) on human turns.\n12\t2. `turnbridge resume [claude|codex]` lists conversations compatible with the\n13\t   current repository state: events anchored to commits reachable from `HEAD`\n14\t   (`--any-commit` lifts this), authored by you or unattributed\n15\t   (`--all` includes collaborators).\n16\t3. Selecting a conversation whose source matches the target CLI delegates to\n17\t   that CLI's native resume.\n18\t4. Cross-CLI selections default to **native fabrication**: turnbridge writes a\n19\t   target-native session file from the canonical events and launches the\n20\t   target's own resume on it. `--bootstrap` (also the automatic fallback when\n21\t   fabrication is unsupported or fails validation) starts a fresh target\n22\t   session whose first prompt instructs it to read the literal transcript.\n23\t\n24\t## Module map\n25\t\n26\t- `src/conversations.ts` — ledger read + grouping + ownership/compat filter.\n27\t- `src/resume.ts` — picker flow, target choice, default-target config.\n28\t- `src/targets/*` — per-CLI `TargetAdapter`: `nativeResume`, `fabricate`,\n29\t  `bootstrap`. Fabrication throws `FabricationUnsupportedError` to trigger the\n30\t  fallback.\n31\t- `src/transcript.ts` + `src/bootstrap.ts` — literal markdown rendering and\n32\t  the rehydration prompt; reports estimated token size before launch.\n33\t- `src/shim.ts` — opt-in PATH shims: bare `claude --resume` / `codex resume`\n34\t  open the merged picker; everything else passes through to the real binary.\n35\t\n36\t## Division of labor with Conversation Ledger\n37\t\n38\tThe ledger owns capture (adapters, cursors, idempotent event ids, git-notes\n39\tstorage, transport). Turnbridge never parses source transcripts itself and\n40\tis a reader plus a writer of *target-native* session files. It writes exactly\n41\tone kind of ledger event — `continuation` lineage edges (below) — and no\n42\tconversation content. Ledger changes made for turnbridge: a library entry\n43\tpoint (`package.json` exports) and human-turn identity stamping.\n44\t\n45\tTurnbridge's reads inherit cledger 0.8.0's read-time behavior: `readEvents()`\n46\tlazily absorbs whatever transport already fetched into the local ref, then\n47\tauto-runs squash/rebase re-anchor detection, which can append a `re_anchor`\n48\tmapping event and print a notice to stderr. Reachability filtering resolves\n49\tthrough `re_anchor` mappings, so a bridged conversation and its `continuation`\n50\tlineage survive a squash merge instead of falling out of the picker. Turnbridge\n51\tissues two such reads per invocation (conversation listing, then lineage\n52\tlookup) and buffers the ledger's stderr notices across both so they render as\n53\tone block above the picker rather than interleaving with it.\n54\t\n55\t## Conversation lineage (branch-on-bridge)\n56\t\n57\tBridging is a fork, not a move. Fabricating a target session writes a real\n58\tnative file; when the user continues there, the target CLI captures it as a\n59\tnew conversation whose id is the session id turnbridge generated (verified:\n60\tCodex resume preserves the id and appends). The source conversation still\n61\texists, so both are resumable — a genuine branch, like a git fork.\n62\t\n63\tTo keep that honest instead of confusing, at fabricate time turnbridge appends\n64\ta `continuation` event (`links: [{rel:\"continues\", target:<source>}]`, content\n65\tcarries `imported_through_seq`) belonging to the target conversation. The\n66\tpicker reads these and labels rows in both directions (`→ bridged to Codex`,\n67\t`↳ continued from Claude Code`); re-bridging a conversation that already\n68\tembeds imported history is allowed but flagged. The default listing shows\n69\tboth branch endpoints — abandonment is not assumed. The same edge is what lets\n70\ta downstream consumer (intent-recall) de-duplicate the copied prefix: target\n71\tturns `0..imported_through_seq` are known copies of the source, not fresh\n72\tevidence, so turnbridge does not need to alter capture to avoid double-count.\n73\t\n74\t## Fabrication contract (per target adapter)\n75\t\n76\t- Pin the CLI versions the writer was validated against; on an unknown version\n77\t  warn and offer bootstrap.\n78\t- Write only new session files under the CLI's own session directory; never\n79\t  modify existing native sessions.\n80\t- Represent foreign tool calls faithfully (or as clearly-labeled text when the\n81\t  target rejects unknown tool schemas — see per-adapter spec notes).\n82\t- Propagate the source model id into fabricated assistant envelopes verbatim\n83\t  (decided 2026-07-21). If the target harness recognizes it — e.g. the user\n84\t  registered matching external models in Claude Code — the session restores\n85\t  seamlessly; otherwise the target warns once and falls back to its default,\n86\t  which is accurate (\"this bridge changed models\"). Never substitute a\n87\t  recognized-but-false id.\n88\t\n89\tReverse-engineered, empirically verified format specs (pinned versions,\n90\tminimal working schemas, unknowns): [docs/specs/claude-session-format.md](specs/claude-session-format.md)\n91\tand [docs/specs/codex-rollout-format.md](specs/codex-rollout-format.md).\n92\tKey constraints: Claude resume is scoped to the project dir encoded from the\n93\tlaunch cwd and reconstructs history by walking `parentUuid` from the last\n94\tline; Codex resume-by-id scans `~/.codex/sessions` for a matching filename +\n95\t`session_meta` id, and only plain `message` response_items are verified-safe\n96\tto replay (foreign tool calls fold into labeled text). Claude accepts foreign\n97\t`tool_use` names verbatim, so Codex→Claude keeps structured tool history.\n98\t\n99\t## Sharing model\n100\t\n101\t`actor.id`/`actor.display` come from `git config user.email/name` at capture\n102\ttime. Default listings: own + unattributed conversations; `--all` shows every\n103\tauthor with labels. Transport is optional-but-default-on: Conversation\n104\tLedger's auto-installed pre-push hook and fetch refspec ride normal\n105\t`git push`/`git fetch`, gated by the ledger's secret scan and disableable per\n106\tuser (`{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}`); `cledger sync`\n107\tremains available for an explicit push/fetch/merge. Turnbridge adds no\n108\tnetwork behavior of its own — it only reads whatever the ledger has already\n109\tabsorbed locally. Events captured before identity stamping existed are\n110\tunattributed; a forced transcript rescan after the upgrade can duplicate\n111\tthose turns under new event ids.\n112\t\n113\t## Risks\n114\t\n115\tNative formats change without notice (fabrication is version-pinned, bootstrap\n116\tis the recovery path); transcript content can include secrets (the ledger\n117\tredacts at capture and sync time, but matching is pattern/entropy-based and\n118\tcannot catch every secret — review before sharing still matters); full\n119\thistories can exceed the target's context (size is reported before launch);\n120\tcrashes mid-turn leave partial capture, which the ledger tolerates by design.\n121\t\n122\t## Roadmap\n123\t\n124\tToward an npm release (decided 2026-07-21; deepen the two existing targets\n125\tbefore broadening):\n126\t\n127\t- Publish `conversation-ledger` to npm (name is unclaimed), then swap the\n128\t  `file:` dependency for a semver range — `prepublishOnly` refuses to publish\n129\t  until this happens. Deliberately deferred while both packages are iterated\n130\t  on and dogfooded for a few days.\n131\t- Adapter-drift automation stage 2: the daily check\n132\t  (`.github/workflows/adapter-drift.yml`) currently opens a tracking issue on\n133\t  drift; the commented-out follow-on job hands the issue to a Claude Code\n134\t  action that revalidates the spec against the new CLI release and drafts the\n135\t  PR. Needs an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` repo secret and comfort with CI pushing\n136\t  branches.\n137\t- Revalidation tooling exists and is the bar for widening version pins:\n138\t  `npm run smoke:interactive` (real-TUI render check, both targets) plus\n139\t  `scripts/probe-codex-content.mjs` (headless model-recall probe).\n140\t- Still-open verification gaps from the spec docs: large/long histories\n141\t  (size cliffs, Codex auto-compaction), timestamp-format tolerance,\n142\t  sessionId/filename mismatch, duplicate or dangling uuid handling.\n143\t- **Encrypted-reasoning replay** *(shipped, needs a turnbridge version bump)*\n144\t  — Codex's `reasoning` items are ciphertext only OpenAI's servers can\n145\t  decrypt; conversation-ledger 0.10.0 preserves them losslessly and opaquely\n146\t  (`kind: \"reasoning\"`, ciphertext in `raw.data`, `content` a bare opacity\n147\t  marker). `listConversations` (`src/conversations.ts`) now fetches\n148\t  `reasoning` events alongside `conversation_turn`s — `readEvents`'s `kind`\n149\t  filter is exact-match only, so it fetches unfiltered and narrows\n150\t  client-side — and carries them through `ConversationSummary.events` in\n151\t  seq order; `turnCount` still counts only visible turns.\n152\t  `src/targets/codex.ts`'s `buildRolloutLines` replays each reasoning\n153\t  event's verbatim `raw.data` response_item line at its correct seq position\n154\t  when `event.producer.source === \"codex\"` — gated per-event, not\n155\t  per-conversation, even though today's data model (a `ConversationSummary`\n156\t  groups by one native session id, hence one source) makes that equivalent\n157\t  to a per-conversation check in practice; genuine multi-hop carry-over\n158\t  (reconstructing an ancestor hop's reasoning via the lineage chain when\n159\t  re-bridging) is deliberately not built and stays a follow-up. Never\n160\t  applies to a Claude Code target: foreign reasoning can't be forged as a\n161\t  native reasoning item, so the only real switch is whether provider-matched\n162\t  replay happens, not what it's coerced into — unreadable/foreign reasoning\n163\t  is still folded into labeled text as before. On by default (decided\n164\t  2026-07-23); opt out with `--no-reasoning-replay` or\n165\t  `{\"reasoningReplay\": false}` in `~/.turnbridge/config.json` (`src/config.ts`).\n166\t  No ownership gate — replay applies to any synced conversation, not just\n167\t  the current user's own, per 2026-07-23 decision. The fabricated session's\n168\t  import notice (`buildRolloutLines`) is conditional: it discloses the\n169\t  replayed-block count when replay happened instead of the blanket \"hidden\n170\t  reasoning ... not transferred\" claim, and still states that claim for\n171\t  everything else in the same session. Empirically verified via\n172\t  scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs (manual injection, independent of\n173\t  this integration): same-account and cross-account replay of a real blob in\n174\t  a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI versions, and a\n175\t  different paid ChatGPT account (identical token accounting), so the\n176\t  ciphertext is not keyed per-account; the free tier cannot run Codex at all.\n177\t  Still open: blob TTL, and whether API-platform-org auth behaves the same\n178\t  as ChatGPT auth — turnbridge attempts replay unconditionally either way\n179\t  since auth mode isn't observable from here; a rejected blob at actual\n180\t  `codex resume` time has no proactive detection and falls under the same\n181\t  `--bootstrap` escape hatch as any other fabrication-drift failure.\n182\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LBjbmBvdpVJ1uhBYxZKkf5","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","dirty_fingerprint":"8e94c8a4de8074d5913d19180e2844cbac51d55933bbb375da42b337fd81df6d","head":"a61d7e48f18594b62ae378267837151b1827b246","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:96801dab-689a-4e0e-a6f4-e8d705f9b9f2","seq":12},"id":"ev1-26be65972022fd60354bb985bf802edcc7fd7b5822ea9d95dd6071f242aeda7b","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-26T00:37:20.996Z","producer":{"session_id":"96801dab-689a-4e0e-a6f4-e8d705f9b9f2","source":"claude-code","tool":"cledger","version":"0.10.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# WIP technical design\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** MVP implemented for Claude Code ⇄ Codex; fabrication adapters are\n4\tversion-pinned and must be revalidated per CLI release.\n5\t\n6\t## Main flow\n7\t\n8\t1. Conversation Ledger's hooks (`cledger install all`) capture each CLI's\n9\t   local transcript incrementally into git notes as normalized\n10\t   `conversation_turn` events, stamped with the repo's git identity\n11\t   (`actor.id` = `user.email`) on human turns.\n12\t2. `turnbridge resume [claude|codex]` lists conversations compatible with the\n13\t   current repository state: events anchored to commits reachable from `HEAD`\n14\t   (`--any-commit` lifts this), authored by you or unattributed\n15\t   (`--all` includes collaborators).\n16\t3. Selecting a conversation whose source matches the target CLI delegates to\n17\t   that CLI's native resume.\n18\t4. Cross-CLI selections default to **native fabrication**: turnbridge writes a\n19\t   target-native session file from the canonical events and launches the\n20\t   target's own resume on it. `--bootstrap` (also the automatic fallback whe